# Go testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to the standard
`testing` package and its modern (1.23-1.25) additions. Applies only to
`*.go` files.

## Layout and naming

- Tests live beside the code they cover as `<file>_test.go` in the same
  package (or `<pkg>_test` for a black-box test that only exercises the
  exported API) — no separate `tests/` tree.
- Table-driven tests with `t.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) { ... })`
  subtests are the default shape for anything with more than one case;
  name each case descriptively, not `case1`/`case2`.
- `testdata/` holds golden files and fixtures a test reads; Go's tooling
  already excludes it from builds — do not invent another fixture
  directory convention.

## Test helpers and lifecycle

- Mark a helper function `t.Helper()` as its first line so failures report
  the caller's line, not the helper's.
- Use `t.Cleanup(func() { ... })` for teardown instead of a manual
  `defer` chain scattered across a subtest — it composes correctly with
  `t.Run` and parallel subtests.
- Use `t.Context()` (Go 1.24+) for a context that is canceled when the
  test completes, instead of hand-rolling `context.WithCancel` plus a
  `defer cancel()` in every test.
- Call `t.Parallel()` deliberately, only when the subtest's fixtures are
  genuinely independent — a parallel subtest sharing mutable fixture
  state races silently.

## Assertions and determinism

- Plain `if got != want { t.Errorf(...) }` (or the project's already-
  configured assertion library) — do not introduce a new assertion
  library into a project that has not adopted one.
- Never synchronize with `time.Sleep` to wait for a goroutine/async result
  — use a channel, `sync.WaitGroup`, `context` deadline, or a polling
  helper with a timeout so the test is deterministic and not flaky under
  load.
- `httptest.NewServer`/`httptest.NewRecorder` for HTTP handler tests
  instead of binding a real port.

## Fuzzing and benchmarks

- `func FuzzX(f *testing.F)` with `f.Add(...)` seed corpus entries for
  parsers, decoders, or anything that touches untrusted input formats;
  run locally with `go test -fuzz=FuzzX` before relying on it in CI.
- Benchmarks use `for b.Loop() { ... }` (Go 1.24+) rather than the older
  `for i := 0; i < b.N; i++`; `b.Loop()` resets the timer automatically
  and keeps loop-body values alive against dead-code elimination, so
  results are not accidentally optimized away.

## Race detection and coverage

- Run `go test -race ./...` for any change touching goroutines, shared
  state, or channels — the race detector catches data races unit
  assertions cannot see.
- New behavior gets a new test in the same change; a bug fix gets a
  regression test that fails before the fix and passes after.
